tion</u>”</td><td><strong>Purpose + precise technical vocabulary</strong></td><td>The purpose is expressed with <strong>to + verb</strong>, and the technical quantity is named precisely.</td></tr><tr><td>“<u class="paper-phrase">is verified through experiments</u>”</td><td><strong>Evidence language</strong></td><td>The claim is connected to <strong>experimental evidence</strong>, not personal opinion.</td></tr></table></div><div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px"><div class="task"><h3>Student Task 1 — Find the Academic Feature</h3><p>For each underlined phrase, decide which feature is strongest:</p><p><strong>formal register · hedging · passive voice · purpose · technical precision · evidence</strong></p><textarea placeholder="Example: could be reduced → hedging + passive voice because..."></textarea></div><div class="task"><h3>Student Task 2 — Build Your Own Version</h3><p>Use the published-language pattern, but write about a different Electrical Engineering context.</p><div class="prompt"><strong>Pattern:</strong> The effectiveness of ______ <u class="paper-phrase">is verified through experiments</u>.</div><textarea placeholder="Example: The effectiveness of the proposed filter is verified through laboratory measurements..."></textarea><div class="prompt"><strong>Pattern:</strong> ______ is used <u class="paper-phrase">to mitigate</u> ______.</div><textarea placeholder="Write your own technical sentence..."></textarea></div></div><div class="takeaway"><strong>Key point:</strong> a real research paper shows that professional Academic English is usually <strong>specific, controlled and evidence-linked</strong>. Students should imitate the <strong>language pattern</strong>, not copy the paper's technical content.</div></section><section id="watchlist"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">Language watchlist</span><h2>Common Issues to Watch For</h2></div><p>Especially useful for Arabic-speaking EFL writers. These are <strong>common transfer patterns</strong>, not problems every student will have.</p></div><div class="grid3 error-grid"><div class="card"><h3>1. Articles</h3><p>Use <strong>the</strong> when the noun is specific or already identified.</p><div class="good"><strong>The</strong> voltage across R1 was measured.</div></div><div class="card"><h3>2. Uncountable Nouns</h3><p><strong>research, equipment, software, information</strong> normally do not take a plural <em>-s</em> in these meanings.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>3. Verb Patterns</h3><p><del>discuss about</del> → <strong>discuss</strong><br><del>explain me</del> → <strong>explain to me</strong></br></p></div><div class="card"><h3>4. Comma Splices</h3><p><del>The voltage dropped, the device failed.</del></p><p><strong>The voltage dropped; the device failed.</strong><br/>or <strong>The device failed because the voltage dropped.</strong></p></div><div class="card"><h3>5. Tense Choice</h3><p><strong>Past</strong> for completed procedures/results: <em>was measured</em>.<br/><strong>Present</strong> for facts and what a figure shows: <em>Figure 2 shows…</em></p></div><div class="card"><h3>6. Noun Order</h3><p>English technical noun groups place modifiers before the main noun:</p><div class="good">a <strong>three-phase induction motor drive</strong></div></div></div><div class="head" style="margin-top:24px"><div><span class="label">Print-ready reference</span><h2>Quick Academic Word Choices</h2></div><p>Choose the word that is <strong>most accurate for the meaning</strong>; do not replace words mechanically.</p></div><table><tr><th>Often too general / informal</th><th>Possible academic / precise alternative</th></tr><tr><td><strong>get</strong></td><td><strong>obtain, receive, produce</strong> — depending on meaning</td></tr><tr><td><strong>a lot of</strong></td><td><strong>numerous</strong> (countable) or, better, an exact value such as <strong>43%</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>thing</strong></td><td><strong>component, parameter, factor, device, variable</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>good</strong></td><td><strong>effective, accurate, stable, within specification</strong> — if evidence supports it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>bad</strong></td><td><strong>degraded, inadequate, unstable, outside specification</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>look at</strong></td><td><strong>examine, analyse, evaluate, investigate</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>big</strong></td><td><strong>high, large, substantial</strong> + an exact value where possible</td></tr><tr><td><strong>nowadays</strong></td><td><strong>currently, in recent years</strong> — when the time reference is accurate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>So / But / And</strong> at sentence start</td><td>When a more formal connector is useful: <strong>Therefore / However / In addition</strong>. Starting with <em>But</em> or <em>And</em> is not automatically wrong.</td></tr></table></section><section class="practice-bank" id="practice"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">Practice bank · classwork + fast-finisher work</span><h2>Week 1 Practice Lab</h2></div><p>Use these tasks to practise the same Week 1 language skills in new Electrical Engineering contexts. Complete the tasks selected in class; unfinished tasks can be used for extra practice.</p></div><div class="takeaway"><strong>Focus:</strong> formal register · precision · objectivity · hedging · sentence control · common error correction.</div><div class="practice-grid" style="margin-top:14px"><div class="task"><span class="practice-badge">Register</span><h3>Activity D — Register Detective</h3><p>For each sentence, write <strong>Acceptable</strong> or <strong>Revise</strong>. If it needs revision, explain the main problem.</p><ol><li>The inverter got really hot during the test.<input placeholder="Acceptable / Revise + reason" type="text"/></li><li>Figure 3 shows the measured output-voltage waveform.<input placeholder="Acceptable / Revise + reason" type="text"/></li><li>We found a huge improvement in the controller.<input placeholder="Acceptable / Revise + reason" type="text"/></li><li>The experiment was conducted at a supply frequency of 50 Hz.<input placeholder="Acceptable / Revise + reason" type="text"/></li><li>A lot of harmonics appeared when the nonlinear load was connected.<input placeholder="Acceptable / Revise + reason" type="text"/></li><li>The result proves that the proposed method is always better.<input placeholder="Acceptable / Revise + reason" type="text"/></li></ol><div class="student-answer-note"